    "definition_tex": "\\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}\nA finite extension $L/{\\mathbb{Q}}$ is Galois when it is normal and separable. Its Galois group consists of field automorphisms of $L$ fixing ${\\mathbb{Q}}$. The inverse problem is\n\\[\n \\forall G\\text{ finite}\\ \\exists L/{\\mathbb{Q}}\\text{ finite Galois}:\\qquad\n \\operatorname{Gal}(L/{\\mathbb{Q}})\\simeq G.\n\\]\nOnly abstract group realization is demanded, not a uniform algorithm, a prescribed polynomial degree, or a regular realization over a rational-function field.\n\\par\\smallskip\\textit{Formulation and concept references:} \\href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.08708}{[S1]}.\n\n\\subsection{Short English statement}\nDoes every finite group occur as the symmetry group of some finite Galois extension of the rationals?\n\n\\subsection{Sources}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[S1]Fariba Ranjbar and Saeed Ranjbar, arXiv:1512.08708 (2015).\n\\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.08708}.\n\\textit{Formulation source.}\n\\item[S2]The Mathieu group M\\_23 is a Galois group over Q.\n\\url{https://arxiv.org/html/2608.08538v1}.\n\\textit{Further reference; not a proof endorsement.}\n\\item[S3]The inverse Galois challenge, part II.\n\\url{https://galoisrepresentations.org/2026/08/16/the-inverse-galois-challenge-part-ii/}.\n\\textit{Further reference; not a proof endorsement.}\n\\end{itemize}",
