    "domain": "computer_science",
    "domain_label": "Computer Science",
    "exact_target": "As clause width grows, must the best deterministic SAT exponent approach exhaustive-search exponent one?",
    "definition_tex": "\\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}\nFor each fixed $k$, let\n\\[\n s_k=\\inf\\{s\\ge0:k\\text{-SAT on }n\\text{ variables is decidable in }\n O(2^{sn}\\operatorname{poly}(|F|))\\text{ deterministic time}\\}.\n\\]\nThe strong exponential time hypothesis is $\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}s_k=1$. Equivalently, for every $\\varepsilon>0$ some fixed clause width $k$ rules out a deterministic $O(2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}\\operatorname{poly}(|F|))$ algorithm. The algorithm may depend on $k$; the claim is not that every fixed-width SAT problem has exponent one.\n\\par\\smallskip\\textit{Formulation and concept references:} \\href{https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcss.2007.07.007}{[S1]}.\n\n\\subsection{Short English statement}\nAs clause width grows, must the best deterministic SAT exponent approach exhaustive-search exponent one?\n\n\\subsection{Sources}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[S1]C. Calabro, R. Impagliazzo, R. Paturi, J. Comput. System Sci. 74(3):367-381, 2008.\n\\url{https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcss.2007.07.007}.\n\\textit{Formulation source.}\n\\item[S2]Strong ETH Holds for Bounded-Depth Resolution over Parities.\n\\url{https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2025/188/}.\n\\textit{Further reference; not a proof endorsement.}\n\\item[S3]Tight (S)ETH-based Lower Bounds for Pseudopolynomial Algorithms for Bin Packing and Multi-Machine Scheduling.\n\\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.12999}.\n\\textit{Further reference; not a proof endorsement.}\n\\end{itemize}",
