    "exact_target": "Are precisely Campana's special projective varieties those whose rational points become dense after a finite field extension?",
    "definition_tex": "\\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}\nFor a rank-one coherent subsheaf $\\mathcal F\\subseteq\\Omega^p_{X_{\\bar K}}$, its Kodaira dimension measures the growth of sections of its reflexive tensor powers, with value $-\\infty$ if none occur. Campana specialness requires $\\kappa(\\mathcal F)<p$ for all $1\\le p\\le\\dim X$ and all such $\\mathcal F$. Potential density means density after some finite field extension. The conjecture is the biconditional\n\\[\n X_{\\bar K}\\text{ special}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\exists L/K\\text{ finite}:\\overline{X(L)}^{\\rm Zar}=X.\n\\]\nBoth directions are required, for every smooth projective geometrically integral number-field variety. Weak specialness and integral points on open varieties are different notions.\n\\par\\smallskip\\textit{Formulation and concept references:} \\href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0701105}{[S1]}.\n\n\\subsection{Short English statement}\nAre precisely Campana's special projective varieties those whose rational points become dense after a finite field extension?\n\n\\subsection{Sources}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[S1]Abramovich, Birational geometry for number theorists.\n\\url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0701105}.\n\\textit{Formulation source.}\n\\end{itemize}",
