    "exact_target": "Can every finite group be realized by a regular Galois cover of the projective line defined over the rationals?",
    "definition_tex": "\\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}\nA finite extension $F/{\\mathbb{Q}}(T)$ is regular over ${\\mathbb{Q}}$ when $F\\cap\\overline{{\\mathbb{Q}}}={\\mathbb{Q}}$ inside an algebraic closure, so there are no new algebraic constants. The question is\n\\[\n \\forall G\\text{ finite}\\ \\exists F/{\\mathbb{Q}}(T)\\text{ finite Galois and regular}:\\quad\n \\operatorname{Gal}(F/{\\mathbb{Q}}(T))\\simeq G.\n\\]\nGeometrically, this asks for a geometrically integral Galois cover of ${\\mathbb{P}}^1_{{\\mathbb{Q}}}$ with group $G$. A realization only over ${\\mathbb{Q}}$ or with a nontrivial constant-field extension does not meet the regular target.\n\\par\\smallskip\\textit{Formulation and concept references:} \\href{https://arxiv.org/html/2512.23615v1}{[S1]}.\n\n\\subsection{Short English statement}\nCan every finite group be realized by a regular Galois cover of the projective line defined over the rationals?\n\n\\subsection{Sources}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[S1]Demeio and Gvirtz-Chen, Galois Realisations of PSL2(Fp\\textasciicircum{}2).\n\\url{https://arxiv.org/html/2512.23615v1}.\n\\textit{Formulation source.}\n\\end{itemize}",
