    "exact_target": "Can every finite portion of every group be modeled by unitary matrices that nearly preserve multiplication while keeping nonidentity elements detectably away from the identity?",
    "definition_tex": "\\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}\nLet $U(d)=\\{U\\in M_d({\\mathbb{C}}):U^*U=I\\}$ and\n$\\|A\\|_{2,d}=(d^{-1}\\operatorname{Tr}(A^*A))^{1/2}$. A group $G$ is hyperlinear in the selected convention if, for every finite $F\\subseteq G$ and ${\\varepsilon}>0$, there is a map $\\phi:F\\to U(d)$ for some $d$ such that\n\\[\n \\|\\phi(g)\\phi(h)-\\phi(gh)\\|_{2,d}<{\\varepsilon},\n \\qquad \\|\\phi(g)-I\\|_{2,d}>\\sqrt2-{\\varepsilon}\\quad(g\\ne e).\n\\]\nThe first condition applies only when $g,h,gh\\in F$, and the second only to elements of $F$. Dimension may depend on $F$ and ${\\varepsilon}$; exact homomorphisms are not required. Determine whether every group admits these finite approximate matrix models.\n\\par\\smallskip\\textit{Formulation and concept references:} \\href{https://arxiv.org/html/2211.10492v3}{[S1]}.\n\n\\subsection{Short English statement}\nCan every finite portion of every group be modeled by unitary matrices that nearly preserve multiplication while keeping nonidentity elements detectably away from the identity?\n\n\\subsection{Sources}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[S1]A. Dogon, Flexible Hilbert-Schmidt Stability versus Hyperlinearity for Property (T) Groups, arXiv:2211.10492v3 (25 August 2023).\n\\url{https://arxiv.org/html/2211.10492v3}.\n\\textit{Formulation source.}\n\\end{itemize}",
