    "exact_target": "Does deleting a proper algebraic exceptional subset make rational-point counts on every Fano variety follow the proposed height asymptotic? The unrestricted version printed in the catalogue has counterexamples.",
    "definition_tex": "\\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}\nA smooth projective variety $X/K$ is Fano when $-K_X$ is ample. Fix an adelic metric on this line bundle, its multiplicative height $H$, and let $\\rho(X)$ denote the rank of the Picard group over $K$. For a nonempty Zariski open $U\\subseteq X$, put\n\\[\n N(U,B)=\\#\\{x\\in U(K):H(x)\\le B\\}.\n\\]\nThe catalogue requests $N(U,B)\\sim cB(\\log B)^{\\rho(X)-1}$ on a suitable open subset, with $c>0$ when that asymptotic is intended. \\textbf{Formulation warning:} this unrestricted closed-exceptional-set version has counterexamples; a primary discussion is \\href{https://arxiv.org/html/2311.04138v3}{[E1]}, Introduction and Proposition 1.4. Modern formulations allow a thin exceptional set and additional hypotheses. Those are different statements, and are not silently substituted here. The original target is retained for traceability, not certified as an open universal conjecture.\n\\par\\smallskip\\textit{Formulation and concept references:} \\href{https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01393829}{[S1]}, \\href{https://arxiv.org/html/2311.04138v3}{[E1]}.\n\n\\subsection{Short English statement}\nDoes deleting a proper algebraic exceptional subset make rational-point counts on every Fano variety follow the proposed height asymptotic? The unrestricted version printed in the catalogue has counterexamples.\n\n\\subsection{Sources}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[S1]J. Franke, Y. I. Manin, Y. Tschinkel, Invent. Math. 95(2):421-435, 1989.\n\\url{https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01393829}.\n\\textit{Formulation source.}\n\\item[E1]Runxuan Gao, The geometric exceptional set in Manin's conjecture for Batyrev and Tschinkel's example, arXiv:2311.04138v3, Introduction and Proposition 1.4.\n\\url{https://arxiv.org/html/2311.04138v3}.\n\\textit{Added primary source: the failure of the closed-exceptional-set formulation and the distinction from thin-set refinements. Consulted 24 September 2026.}\n\\end{itemize}",
