    "exact_target": "Does every Hamiltonian motion on a closed symplectic manifold leave at least as many points fixed as any smooth real function on that manifold must have critical points?",
    "definition_tex": "\\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}\nA symplectic manifold $(M,\\omega)$ carries a closed nondegenerate two-form. A Hamiltonian diffeomorphism is the time-one map of a time-dependent vector field $X_{H_t}$ satisfying $\\iota_{X_{H_t}}\\omega=-{\\,\\mathrm{d}} H_t$. For a closed connected $M$, put\n\\[\n \\operatorname{Crit}(M)=\\min_{f\\in C^\\infty(M,{\\mathbb{R}})}\\#\\{x:{\\,\\mathrm{d}} f(x)=0\\}.\n\\]\nThe selected assertion is $\\#\\operatorname{Fix}(\\phi)\\ge\\operatorname{Crit}(M)$ for every Hamiltonian diffeomorphism $\\phi$. This minimum permits degenerate critical points; it is not the minimum restricted to Morse functions. Likewise the fixed points need not be nondegenerate. An infinite fixed-point set satisfies the cardinal lower bound automatically.\n\\par\\smallskip\\textit{Formulation and concept references:} \\href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.09192}{[S1]}.\n\n\\subsection{Short English statement}\nDoes every Hamiltonian motion on a closed symplectic manifold leave at least as many points fixed as any smooth real function on that manifold must have critical points?\n\n\\subsection{Sources}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[S1]Formal statement, abstract.\n\\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.09192}.\n\\textit{Formulation source.}\n\\end{itemize}",
    "definition_file": "attacks/open_problems/top_problems/30006772.tex",
    "link": null,
