    "exact_target": "Can an algorithm compute a guaranteed denominator cutoff for every exceptionally accurate rational approximation to a specified algebraic irrational?",
    "definition_tex": "\\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}\nEncode a real algebraic irrational $\\alpha$ by its integer minimal polynomial and a rational interval containing exactly the chosen real root. Given also rational ${\\varepsilon}>0$, the requested single total algorithm must output an integer $Q\\ge1$ such that\n\\[\n \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\quad q\\ge1,\\quad\n |\\alpha-p/q|<q^{-2-{\\varepsilon}}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q\\le Q.\n\\]\nThe algorithm must halt on every valid input; $p$ may be any integer. The bound can be enormous and the running time unrestricted. Merely proving that finitely many such approximations exist, or giving a bound depending on an unspecified noncomputable constant, does not supply this effective version.\n\\par\\smallskip\\textit{Formulation and concept references:} \\href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1601.02243v1}{[S1]}.\n\n\\subsection{Short English statement}\nCan an algorithm compute a guaranteed denominator cutoff for every exceptionally accurate rational approximation to a specified algebraic irrational?\n\n\\subsection{Sources}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[S1]Effective Approximation and Diophantine Applications; arXiv1601.02243v1,10January2016.\n\\url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1601.02243v1}.\n\\textit{Formulation source.}\n\\end{itemize}",
