    "exact_target": "Is the moonshine vertex operator algebra uniquely determined by strong rationality, holomorphicity, central charge twenty-four, and the absence of weight-one states?",
    "definition_tex": "\\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}\nA vertex operator algebra has a graded vector space, vacuum and conformal vectors, and vertex operators $Y(a,z)=\\sum_{m\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}}a_mz^{-m-1}$ satisfying the usual vacuum, translation and Jacobi axioms. Here CFT type means $V=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}V_n$ and $V_0={\\mathbb{C}}\\mathbf1$. The subspace $C_2(V)$ is spanned by $a_{-2}b$; $C_2$-cofiniteness means $V/C_2(V)$ is finite-dimensional. Strong rationality also requires simplicity, self-contragredience and complete reducibility of admissible modules. Holomorphic means $V$ is its only irreducible module. The target is\n\\[\n c(V)=24,\\quad V_1=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad V\\cong V^{\\natural}\n\\]\nfor every VOA satisfying those hypotheses, where $V^{\\natural}$ is the standard moonshine VOA. Matching its character alone is not the requested isomorphism.\n\\par\\smallskip\\textit{Formulation and concept references:} \\href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.18166}{[S1]}.\n\n\\subsection{Short English statement}\nIs the moonshine vertex operator algebra uniquely determined by strong rationality, holomorphicity, central charge twenty-four, and the absence of weight-one states?\n\n\\subsection{Sources}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[S1]Equivalence relations on vertex operator algebras II, arXiv:2410.18166v2.\n\\url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.18166}.\n\\textit{Formulation source.}\n\\end{itemize}",
