    "exact_target": "What is the exact rate for reliably transmitting quantum information through a qubit depolarizing channel using arbitrary block codes but no extra shared resources?",
    "definition_tex": "\\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}\nFor a qubit state $\\rho$, the depolarizing channel is\n$\\mathcal D_p(\\rho)=(1-p)\\rho+pI_2/2$, $0\\le p\\le1$. A block code uses a completely positive trace-preserving encoder into $n$ qubits and a decoder after $\\mathcal D_p^{\\otimes n}$. A rate $R$ is achievable if $k_n/n\\to R$ and the entanglement-transmission error for $k_n$ logical qubits tends to zero. The unassisted quantum capacity is the supremum of achievable rates. Determine\n\\[\n Q(\\mathcal D_p)\\quad\\text{for every }p\\in[0,1].\n\\]\nNo preshared entanglement or auxiliary classical messages are available. The parameter $p$ is the replacement-by-maximally-mixed-state probability in this formula, not another convention's total Pauli-error probability.\n\\par\\smallskip\\textit{Formulation and concept references:} \\href{https://www.preskill.caltech.edu/ph219/chap10_6A_2025.pdf}{[S1]}.\n\n\\subsection{Short English statement}\nWhat is the exact rate for reliably transmitting quantum information through a qubit depolarizing channel using arbitrary block codes but no extra shared resources?\n\n\\subsection{Sources}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[S1]Quantum Information chapter10, 2025 snapshot.\n\\url{https://www.preskill.caltech.edu/ph219/chap10_6A_2025.pdf}.\n\\textit{Formulation source.}\n\\end{itemize}",
