    "exact_target": "Must every finitely generated subgroup of a CAT(0) group either be solvable up to finite index or contain a nonabelian free group?",
    "definition_tex": "\\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}\nA CAT(0) space is a geodesic metric space whose geodesic triangles are no thicker than Euclidean comparison triangles. It is proper if closed bounded sets are compact. A group action by isometries is proper if only finitely many translates of a compact set meet that set, and cocompact if the quotient is compact. Suppose $G$ admits such an action on a proper CAT(0) space. The target is the alternative\n\\[\n H\\le G,\\ H\\text{ finitely generated}\n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n [H\\text{ virtually solvable}\\ \\text{or}\\ F_2\\hookrightarrow H].\n\\]\nHere virtually solvable means having a solvable subgroup of finite index, and $F_2$ is the free group on two generators. No cubical structure, finite-dimensionality, or geodesic completeness of the space is assumed in the recorded question.\n\\par\\smallskip\\textit{Formulation and concept references:} \\href{https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/forum-of-mathematics-pi/article/tits-alternative-for-2dimensional-mathrm-cat0-complexes/E1B5B96E5D4324F2F3707B4A05B2D86C}{[S1]}.\n\n\\subsection{Short English statement}\nMust every finitely generated subgroup of a CAT(0) group either be solvable up to finite index or contain a nonabelian free group?\n\n\\subsection{Sources}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[S1]Osajda and Przytycki, Tits Alternative for 2-dimensional CAT(0) complexes.\n\\url{https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/forum-of-mathematics-pi/article/tits-alternative-for-2dimensional-mathrm-cat0-complexes/E1B5B96E5D4324F2F3707B4A05B2D86C}.\n\\textit{Formulation source.}\n\\end{itemize}",
