    "domain": "computer_science",
    "domain_label": "Computer Science",
    "exact_target": "Does every synchronizing $n$-state deterministic automaton have a reset word of length at most $(n-1)^2$?",
    "definition_tex": "\\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}\nA complete deterministic finite automaton consists of a finite state set $Q$, finite alphabet $\\Sigma$, and transition function $\\delta:Q\\times\\Sigma\\to Q$, extended to words by successive transitions. A word $w\\in\\Sigma^*$ resets the automaton if $\\delta(q,w)$ is the same state for every $q\\in Q$. The automaton is synchronizing if some reset word exists. For $n=|Q|$, \\v{C}ern\\'y's conjecture asserts\n\\[\n \\min\\{|w|:w\\text{ resets the automaton}\\}\\le(n-1)^2.\n\\]\nThe empty word is permitted and resets a one-state automaton. The assertion is about every synchronizing complete automaton, with no strong-connectivity or alphabet-size restriction. A polynomial algorithm finding some reset word does not prove this particular sharp quadratic length bound.\n\\par\\smallskip\\textit{Formulation and concept references:} \\href{https://arxiv.org/html/2508.15655}{[S1]}.\n\n\\subsection{Short English statement}\nDoes every synchronizing $n$-state deterministic automaton have a reset word of length at most $(n-1)^2$?\n\n\\subsection{Sources}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[S1]Mikhail V. Volkov, \"List of Results on the \u010cern\u00fd Conjecture and Reset Thresholds for Synchronizing Automata,\" arXiv:2508.15655 (2025, updated August 2026).\n\\url{https://arxiv.org/html/2508.15655}.\n\\textit{Formulation source.}\n\\end{itemize}",
