        "sections": {
          "preamble": "\\section{MO 363120: ``Players alternate moving a $\\{\\swarrow,\\uparrow,\\rightarrow\\}$ piece on a chessboard''}\n\\label{sec:mo363120}\n\\noindent\\textbf{MathOverflow link:} \\url{https://mathoverflow.net/questions/363120/players-alternate-moving-a-swarrow-uparrow-rightarrow-piece-on-a-chessb} (accessed 2026-01-16).\n\n\\subsection*{1) FORMAL RESTATEMENT}\nFix $k\\in\\NN$. Consider the board\n\\[\nB_k := \\{0,1,\\dots,2k\\}\\times\\{0,1,\\dots,2k\\} \\subset \\ZZ^2.\n\\]\nA single token starts at a specified square $(x_0,y_0)\\in B_k$.\nTwo players alternate moves; a move from $(x,y)$ may go to any of:\n\\[\n(x-1,y-1),\\quad (x,y+1),\\quad (x+1,y),\n\\]\nprovided the destination lies in $B_k$ and has not been visited previously by the token.\nThe first player who cannot move loses (normal play).\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{Question.}\nDescribe the set of losing starting squares (for the first player), i.e.\n\\[\nL_k := \\{(x,y)\\in B_k: \\text{the first player loses with optimal play starting at }(x,y)\\}.\n\\]\nThe MO post reports experimental evidence that all even--even squares (both coordinates even) are losing, with a small number of additional exceptions.\n\n\\subsection*{2) QUICK LITERATURE/CONTEXT CHECK}\nThe MathOverflow page has no posted answers as of 2026-01-16.\nThe post mentions an equivalent formulation in terms of a divisor game on divisors of $10^{2k}$ (reachable positions correspond to divisors and moves correspond to multiplying/dividing by $2,5,10$), but no general solution is provided.\n\n\\subsection*{3) ATTACK PLAN}\n\\textbf{Proof-track ideas.}\n\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]\n\\item Try to find a pairing/mirroring strategy proving that a large class of starts are losing (e.g. all even--even).\n\\item Seek an invariant or monotone potential to show that certain starts are winning by forcing a move to a losing class.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\textbf{Disproof-track ideas.}\n\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]\n\\item Compute $L_k$ for small $k$ by exact minimax recursion (state includes visited set). Compare with the conjectured description.\n\\item Attempt to produce a $k$ where some even--even start is actually winning.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nWe carried out the exact minimax computation for $k=1,2,3$.\n\n\\subsection*{4) WORK}\n\\subsubsection*{4.1 Exact computation for small boards}\nWe solved the game by recursion on the state $(\\text{current square},\\ \\text{visited set})$ with memoization.\nFor each start square, visited set initially contains only the start.\nA state is winning iff there exists a legal move to a losing state.\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{Results.}\nWrite coordinates as $(x,y)$ with $0\\le x,y\\le 2k$.\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]\n\\item For $k=1$ (board $3\\times3$):\n\\[\nL_1 = \\{(0,0),(0,2),(2,0),(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\nThese are exactly the even--even squares.\n\\item For $k=2$ (board $5\\times5$): $L_2$ consists of the $9$ even--even squares $(x,y)$ with $x,y\\in\\{0,2,4\\}$.\n\\item For $k=3$ (board $7\\times7$): $L_3$ contains all $16$ even--even squares $(x,y)$ with $x,y\\in\\{0,2,4,6\\}$, and additionally\n\\[\n(3,4),(4,5),(5,0),(5,6).\n\\]\n\\end{itemize}\nThus the heuristic ``even--even squares are losing'' is consistent with these computations, but it is not a complete description once $k\\ge 3$.\n\n\\subsubsection*{4.2 Partial structural observations}\nThe move set contains directed $3$-cycles\n\\[\n(x,y)\\to(x+1,y)\\to(x+1,y+1)\\to(x,y),\n\\]\nso the underlying directed graph is not acyclic; the no-revisit constraint is essential.\nThe game is a special instance of \\emph{vertex geography} on a directed graph, a class known to be computationally hard in general.\n\n\\subsection*{5) VERIFICATION}\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]\n\\item Computation was exact for $k\\le 3$ (state spaces: $9,25,49$ vertices; still manageable under memoization).\n\\item No general theorem was proved; no claim is made beyond the computed values.\n\\item The additional losing squares for $k=3$ show that ``even--even iff losing'' is false in general.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{6) FINAL}\n\\begin{center}\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED}\n\\end{center}"
        },
